Source: fips-initramfs
Section: net
Priority: optional
Maintainer: Johnathan Corgan <johnathan@corganlabs.com>
Build-Depends: debhelper-compat (= 13),
               ca-certificates,
               curl
Standards-Version: 4.7.0
Rules-Requires-Root: no
Homepage: https://github.com/jmcorgan/fips-initramfs

Package: fips-initramfs
Architecture: any
Depends: ${misc:Depends},
         ${shlibs:Depends},
         initramfs-tools (>= 0.120) | initramfs-tools-core,
         dropbear-initramfs
Recommends: cryptsetup-initramfs
Suggests: fips
Description: FIPS mesh node in the initramfs for remote unlock
 Starts a FIPS mesh node during early boot and binds dropbear to the
 resulting fips0 interface, so an encrypted root filesystem can be
 unlocked over the mesh rather than over the local IP network.
 .
 The node runs under a persistent identity generated at install time, so
 its mesh address is fixed and is known before the machine boots. The
 address is written into the generated image, and the boot scripts refuse
 to report success if the node comes up at a different one.
 .
 Console passphrase entry is never disabled. Every failure in this path
 leaves the operator at the ordinary cryptsetup prompt.
 .
 The package carries the fips daemon and fipsctl, taken from a published
 FIPS release, so it needs nothing else installed to work. The booted
 system runs no FIPS daemon of its own: the node exists only inside the
 initramfs, for the length of the unlock.
 .
 A machine that also has the fips package installed uses that package's
 binaries instead, so the node in the initramfs is the same build as the
 daemon the booted system runs.
